        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Measure before you decide.</strong> A hygrometer settles it in a week, and most heated winter basements read 30 to 45 percent — dry enough that the machine is doing nothing but consuming electricity.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If the readings do call for action, match the machine to the temperature: a standard compressor unit frosts up below about 15°C, so a cold basement, garage or crawlspace needs a low-temperature model with active defrost or a desiccant unit.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And check what is actually producing the moisture first. Ground moisture through a bare crawlspace floor, condensation on uninsulated pipes and rim joists, and water arriving from a blocked gutter outside are all cheaper and more permanently fixed at the source than managed with an appliance all winter.
          </p>
